1
00:00:02,653 --> 00:00:06,856
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a common practice that I hear about is like this.

2
00:00:10,138 --> 00:00:22,766
[SPEAKER_01]: A person wants to practice equanimity, wants to have this, it sounds quite nice, equanimity, not being bothered by things, always peaceful.

3
00:00:23,586 --> 00:00:29,550
[SPEAKER_01]: But, but it is because they are not peaceful, they are bothered, right?

4
00:00:30,451 --> 00:00:30,711
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

5
00:00:32,761 --> 00:00:37,643
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, well, I can practice these four brahma vihāras, basically, you know, metta, and so on.

6
00:00:37,663 --> 00:00:43,026
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sometimes I do metta, sometimes I do karuṇa, sometimes upekka, sometimes mudita.

7
00:00:44,286 --> 00:00:48,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Depends on how I feel, or what's attractive at the moment.

8
00:00:49,989 --> 00:00:52,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so, because I can't just do metta all the time.

9
00:00:52,150 --> 00:00:52,790
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a bit boring.

10
00:00:53,650 --> 00:00:55,871
[SPEAKER_01]: But, so, equanimity is quite a nice one.

11
00:00:56,972 --> 00:00:57,472
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh...

12
00:00:59,038 --> 00:01:00,379
[SPEAKER_01]: And they go about it like this.

13
00:01:00,419 --> 00:01:22,862
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I feel bothered by somebody, and then I go away, and I sit by myself, and I do this equanimity practice, by imagining this person, the situation, and just trying to invoke a sense, an energy of equanimity, of

14
00:01:25,178 --> 00:01:31,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Of just like, I'm okay with this person who's irritating me.

15
00:01:32,505 --> 00:01:37,030
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm okay with the situation that's absolutely, you know, making me so angry.

16
00:01:39,012 --> 00:01:49,642
[SPEAKER_01]: So they just, you know, just kind of trying to push this equanimous feeling into them, into their minds, into their perception of it.

17
00:01:51,986 --> 00:01:52,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there something wrong with that?

18
00:01:52,947 --> 00:01:55,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Or can a person just keep on doing that?

19
00:01:55,388 --> 00:01:56,088
[SPEAKER_01]: It's hard, you know.

20
00:01:56,108 --> 00:01:58,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there are a few things wrong with it.

21
00:01:59,690 --> 00:02:12,098
[SPEAKER_00]: The two main points, two main mistakes are... Well, first of all, it's not that you're not equanimous because you haven't practiced Brahma-Vihāras.

22
00:02:12,918 --> 00:02:17,821
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not equanimous because you're still liable to be disturbed.

23
00:02:18,813 --> 00:02:23,336
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you don't know how to remain unaffected even before you were disturbed.

24
00:02:24,577 --> 00:02:25,657
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind is not developed.

25
00:02:26,338 --> 00:02:32,442
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have no choice but to experience the disturbed mind when something agitates it and disturbs it.

26
00:02:32,562 --> 00:02:33,723
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know how to prevent that.

27
00:02:34,883 --> 00:02:42,108
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the only thing you know is how to then cover it up or try to... try to reshape it or... or whatever practice you might be doing.

28
00:02:42,408 --> 00:02:47,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, the only way you know is to try to deal with it.

29
00:02:47,791 --> 00:02:48,472
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a problem.

30
00:02:49,063 --> 00:02:52,905
[SPEAKER_00]: See, the issue is not that you have not found a perfect way of dealing with it.

31
00:02:53,885 --> 00:02:57,247
[SPEAKER_00]: You might well have found an excellent way of dealing with it, but that was never the problem.

32
00:02:57,267 --> 00:03:00,409
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem was that you were affected in the first place, as I keep saying.

33
00:03:01,889 --> 00:03:06,712
[SPEAKER_00]: And the true equanimity is when you can't be affected in the first place, then what is there to deal with?

34
00:03:07,132 --> 00:03:07,953
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the true peace.

35
00:03:08,053 --> 00:03:11,574
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to do anything on account of this, because I'm not bothered.

36
00:03:12,255 --> 00:03:12,875
[SPEAKER_00]: I am peaceful.

37
00:03:13,394 --> 00:03:24,263
[SPEAKER_00]: If I have to do this, if I have to do brahmikhāra, if I have to do that, if I have to do samatha, if I have to do any practices, because I'm bothered, means that's not equanimity, even if I calm myself down with these practices.

38
00:03:25,264 --> 00:03:29,808
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's the first problem, really, and kind of the the fundamental one.

39
00:03:30,629 --> 00:03:36,374
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, really, you want to ask yourself, like, why am I disturbed in the first place?

40
00:03:36,414 --> 00:03:38,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Why can I be disturbed in the first place?

41
00:03:38,235 --> 00:03:40,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you obviously don't want a disturbance.

42
00:03:40,738 --> 00:03:40,998
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet,

43
00:03:42,582 --> 00:03:43,283
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't help it.

44
00:03:44,103 --> 00:03:52,369
[SPEAKER_00]: When you experience something that's challenging, annoying, unpleasant, the mind just gets disturbed.

45
00:03:53,650 --> 00:03:54,831
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do you learn from that?

46
00:03:55,631 --> 00:03:58,514
[SPEAKER_00]: You learn that your own mind doesn't listen to you.

47
00:03:59,534 --> 00:04:01,616
[SPEAKER_00]: You want it to not get disturbed.

48
00:04:01,956 --> 00:04:03,557
[SPEAKER_00]: You want it to remain peaceful.

49
00:04:04,678 --> 00:04:05,579
[SPEAKER_00]: But it ignores you.

50
00:04:05,739 --> 00:04:07,320
[SPEAKER_00]: It just goes and does it anyway.

51
00:04:08,901 --> 00:04:11,343
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you try to paste the practices over.

52
00:04:12,386 --> 00:04:13,448
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your second problem.

53
00:04:13,588 --> 00:04:16,032
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't see what happened there already.

54
00:04:16,052 --> 00:04:16,793
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that was the issue.

55
00:04:17,474 --> 00:04:19,217
[SPEAKER_00]: The issue was not even that you were disturbed.

56
00:04:20,398 --> 00:04:23,022
[SPEAKER_00]: The issue was that your own mind is not listening to you.

57
00:04:23,263 --> 00:04:25,667
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sooner or later, something will highlight that.

58
00:04:26,817 --> 00:04:28,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Something will make that apparent to you.

59
00:04:28,998 --> 00:04:30,178
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when you are disturbed.

60
00:04:30,198 --> 00:04:31,818
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when you are not equanimous.

61
00:04:32,619 --> 00:04:47,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of looking at the root of that, and what causes the mind to not listen to you, you just want to cover up the fact that mind doesn't listen to you, that it's causing you pain, and now you just want to cover up the pain, with a... with a... like a shoving the... the... the square shape into a circle.

62
00:04:48,465 --> 00:04:59,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, do your brahmavihāras, do equanimity, do metta, just so that this pain goes away on account of the mind that doesn't listen to me, and that will continue not listening to me, because I'm not doing anything on that field.

63
00:05:02,570 --> 00:05:04,371
[SPEAKER_01]: So, also, what's...

64
00:05:06,412 --> 00:05:08,973
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that you're not saying that brahmavihāra is wrong.

65
00:05:09,633 --> 00:05:11,153
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I'm saying that...

66
00:05:11,273 --> 00:05:12,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Completely, yeah, yeah.

67
00:05:12,154 --> 00:05:15,155
[SPEAKER_00]: So, brahmavihāra is the actual practice

68
00:05:16,025 --> 00:05:17,266
[SPEAKER_00]: ...of taming your mind.

69
00:05:17,906 --> 00:05:20,527
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's in the complete opposite direction of how people do it.

70
00:05:21,527 --> 00:05:36,654
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order to take... in order to develop brahma-vihāra, first you need to see your own mind as that thing that you are paired with, that doesn't listen to you, that needs taming, that needs containment, and develop clarity in regard to that.

71
00:05:37,374 --> 00:05:40,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, that can take you in a direction... direction of actual brahma-vihāras.

72
00:05:42,650 --> 00:05:44,711
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not something you have to be, like, doing it.

73
00:05:44,791 --> 00:05:50,513
[SPEAKER_00]: You could just be amplifying these aspects of taming the mind that then result in brahmavihāras, certain frameworks.

74
00:05:51,373 --> 00:05:55,414
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unless you have a right view, unless you have seen the signs of your mind, i.e.

75
00:05:55,514 --> 00:06:00,396
[SPEAKER_00]: seen the mind as a phenomenon, seen the mind as that thing that needs taming,

76
00:06:01,796 --> 00:06:12,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless you have developed a degree of kind of un-identification with your own mind, any, basically, practice of brahma-vihāra you do is just a practice of your wrong view on the pretext of brahma-vihāras.

77
00:06:12,605 --> 00:06:13,986
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not what brahma-vihāras are.

78
00:06:16,088 --> 00:06:16,728
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, imagine.

79
00:06:16,968 --> 00:06:18,590
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the dwelling of the brahmas.

80
00:06:19,951 --> 00:06:28,438
[SPEAKER_00]: It takes a bit more to dwell like a brahma than to just have positive thoughts about your fellow men, which anybody can do, and you don't need a Buddha to teach you that.

81
00:06:29,511 --> 00:06:30,672
[SPEAKER_00]: It takes a bit more effort.

82
00:06:30,712 --> 00:06:37,097
[SPEAKER_00]: It takes a bit more of commitment, endurance, and mental kind of elevation, basically.

83
00:06:38,198 --> 00:06:39,058
[SPEAKER_00]: It takes more depth.

84
00:06:39,879 --> 00:06:41,600
[SPEAKER_00]: And that depth begins with the right view.

85
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:53,629
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the right view, in this case, is found in the direction of recognizing, hold on, I have a meditation technique, I do brahmavihāras, I go on the retreats, and it really helps my life.

86
00:06:53,829 --> 00:06:55,050
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm a much calmer person.

87
00:06:55,511 --> 00:06:58,493
[SPEAKER_00]: But, do I actually know

88
00:06:59,480 --> 00:07:06,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I have confidence in the knowledge of being undisturbed to begin with?

89
00:07:07,767 --> 00:07:13,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do I just have confidence that sooner or later I will get my retreat, I will do my technique, and I will calm down?

90
00:07:15,473 --> 00:07:16,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Which one is it?

91
00:07:17,775 --> 00:07:22,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I know how, factually, to remain undisturbed in face of disturbances?

92
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:27,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I have confidence that this wild mind of mine will not turn against me?

93
00:07:28,133 --> 00:07:29,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I've tamed it.

94
00:07:30,254 --> 00:07:31,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I know how to speak to it.

95
00:07:32,515 --> 00:07:38,377
[SPEAKER_00]: I see its signs and features, and I prevent it from going into the wrong pastures.

96
00:07:38,397 --> 00:07:43,139
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, I have absolutely no idea what any of that means, and I just sort of calm myself through focusing practices.

97
00:07:45,640 --> 00:07:47,041
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the beginning, basically.

98
00:07:47,061 --> 00:07:51,423
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your crossroads to realize you can't be practicing both.

99
00:07:52,433 --> 00:08:05,584
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't be going in the direction of digging up the reasons that your mind doesn't listen to you, restraining them, purifying them, and at the same time engaging in avoiding pain that the mind... that the wild mind causes you, through... through focusing and through calming and so on.

100
00:08:09,007 --> 00:08:10,108
[SPEAKER_00]: They're mutually exclusive.

101
00:08:13,070 --> 00:08:18,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Because each time you act out of avoiding pain that that wild mind calls you, you're feeding its wildness.

102
00:08:19,255 --> 00:08:20,837
[SPEAKER_00]: You're feeding its disobedience.

103
00:08:22,302 --> 00:08:29,727
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just trying to deal with the pain of it, ignoring the fact that pain was really not the problem.

104
00:08:29,767 --> 00:08:36,631
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem was the mind that has the ability and power to turn against you and you can't stop it.

105
00:08:37,151 --> 00:08:38,012
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't do anything about it.

106
00:08:38,592 --> 00:08:46,737
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can actually see that even in, like, day-to-day examples of your life, not even necessarily some extreme scenarios where the mind turns against you.

107
00:08:47,117 --> 00:08:51,420
[SPEAKER_00]: You could just see that, like, sometimes, for example, one thing happens to you and you're not bothered.

108
00:08:52,646 --> 00:08:59,789
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind doesn't really latch on it, doesn't pick on it, does not start nagging you and causing you that mental turmoil.

109
00:09:00,450 --> 00:09:03,891
[SPEAKER_00]: On another occasion, something much smaller than that happens.

110
00:09:04,812 --> 00:09:08,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, because of this or that, the mind won't let go of it.

111
00:09:08,314 --> 00:09:11,315
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't sleep at night, and you're filled with whatever.

112
00:09:11,335 --> 00:09:16,258
[SPEAKER_00]: It might be anger, any form of hindrance, anything, because this mind just won't let go of it.

113
00:09:16,418 --> 00:09:17,678
[SPEAKER_00]: And you would want to let go of it.

114
00:09:17,998 --> 00:09:19,199
[SPEAKER_00]: You would want to be peaceful.

115
00:09:20,260 --> 00:09:26,303
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you measure your peace, whether it's the pain I'm feeling, or the pleasure, you're ignoring that that was never the problem.

116
00:09:26,323 --> 00:09:27,584
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the result of the problem.

117
00:09:27,624 --> 00:09:29,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Problem of an untamed mind.

118
00:09:32,426 --> 00:09:42,792
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have untamed mind, and then try to pace Brahma Viharas, or any other Buddhist practice, without developing the right view in terms of taming the mind, none of it will work.

119
00:09:43,072 --> 00:09:45,973
[SPEAKER_00]: And all of that will become a misrepresentation of the dhamma.

120
00:09:47,574 --> 00:09:49,215
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you think you're practicing the dhamma, while you're not.

121
00:09:51,273 --> 00:09:54,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, that's something you used to... you've mentioned before, it's like...

122
00:09:54,755 --> 00:10:04,782
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know if I'm getting it right, but it's like if you got cancer, and because of that cancer you have a headache, and so you take a tablet.

123
00:10:05,839 --> 00:10:05,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

124
00:10:06,319 --> 00:10:10,583
[SPEAKER_01]: And it gets rid of the pain of, you know... And you think you're curing cancer.

125
00:10:11,024 --> 00:10:13,826
[SPEAKER_01]: But, oh, yeah, you do... you're just kind of numbing the pain for a while.

126
00:10:13,866 --> 00:10:14,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

127
00:10:14,867 --> 00:10:18,610
[SPEAKER_01]: And... but if I keep on... if I keep on taking the headache tablet... Cancer will disappear.

128
00:10:18,650 --> 00:10:24,355
[SPEAKER_01]: ...the Brahma Vihāra practice of wishing everyone well, then I won't get angry anymore.

129
00:10:24,455 --> 00:10:24,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

130
00:10:24,655 --> 00:10:30,440
[SPEAKER_00]: That's kind of exactly... exactly the implicit implication by... like most people that engage in these practices, is that

131
00:10:31,201 --> 00:10:41,710
[SPEAKER_00]: I know it's not adequate, but there is this kind of concealed hope in it, that if I just keep covering my pain long enough, one day it just won't be there anymore.

132
00:10:43,412 --> 00:10:49,757
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I keep taking the pills and never allow headache to come up, one day I'll be cancer-free.

133
00:10:51,759 --> 00:10:55,322
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, that doesn't work even in mundane terms, and you know it.

134
00:10:55,862 --> 00:10:58,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Let alone when it comes to freeing your mind and reaching nibbāna.

135
00:11:07,226 --> 00:11:10,890
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if sometimes symptoms are too much, yes, take the headache pill.

136
00:11:11,090 --> 00:11:11,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Calm down.

137
00:11:11,771 --> 00:11:13,872
[SPEAKER_00]: But don't confuse that for treating the illness.

138
00:11:14,913 --> 00:11:19,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is not having headache in the first place on account of your mental cancer.

139
00:11:21,399 --> 00:11:25,683
[SPEAKER_00]: And in this case being mind that can turn against you.

140
00:11:25,703 --> 00:11:26,243
[SPEAKER_00]: At its whim.

141
00:11:26,604 --> 00:11:29,026
[SPEAKER_00]: If something prompts it, if circumstances change, if

142
00:11:30,062 --> 00:11:41,749
[SPEAKER_00]: If you didn't get enough sleep, not enough food or something, that that mind becomes agitated and sort of touchy and it turns against you and it starts causing you trouble, basically.

143
00:11:42,129 --> 00:11:44,050
[SPEAKER_00]: Your own thinking is not in your control.

144
00:11:44,370 --> 00:11:50,434
[SPEAKER_00]: It starts annoying you, harassing you, bombarding you with stuff, causing you pain, disturbing you and you can't stop it.

145
00:11:52,215 --> 00:11:57,698
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will never stop it for as long as you don't start basically admitting that that's really the root of the problem.

146
00:11:58,167 --> 00:12:01,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Regardless of what pain might come out of it, so don't be just trying to act out of...

147
00:12:01,608 --> 00:12:02,769
[SPEAKER_00]: I just want to get rid of the pain.

148
00:12:02,829 --> 00:12:03,169
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

149
00:12:03,809 --> 00:12:07,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Put the symptoms aside for a while, so that you can discern the illness.

150
00:12:08,551 --> 00:12:13,233
[SPEAKER_00]: In this case, illness is, yeah, it's my own mind simply not listening to my commands.

151
00:12:14,913 --> 00:12:15,453
[SPEAKER_00]: And why is that?

152
00:12:15,493 --> 00:12:16,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because you never trained it.

153
00:12:17,214 --> 00:12:20,795
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you just take a random street dog and start telling it to sit.

154
00:12:21,636 --> 00:12:22,396
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not going to do it.

155
00:12:22,796 --> 00:12:24,517
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not impossible to teach it either.

156
00:12:24,957 --> 00:12:26,097
[SPEAKER_00]: But you do have to make the effort.

157
00:12:26,217 --> 00:12:27,478
[SPEAKER_00]: So you have to recognize, okay, that's the

158
00:12:28,454 --> 00:12:29,775
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what he keeps talking about, the training.

159
00:12:30,636 --> 00:12:32,357
[SPEAKER_00]: The dog training of your own mind.

160
00:12:34,959 --> 00:12:45,067
[SPEAKER_00]: And once the mind is trained, as the suttas often describe, like, arahant is like an unmovable mountain, regardless of the weather that's hitting it, and falling, and the rain, and the wind.

161
00:12:46,288 --> 00:12:51,432
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no amount of circumstances will make a trained mind turn against itself.

162
00:12:53,473 --> 00:12:56,556
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, no amount of circumstances will make you suffer irrationally.

163
00:12:56,656 --> 00:12:58,037
[SPEAKER_00]: And if it's turning against itself,

164
00:12:59,722 --> 00:13:04,586
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not trained enough, or it's not trained at all, depending on how badly it turns against itself.

165
00:13:06,067 --> 00:13:08,389
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to find out how to train the mind?

166
00:13:08,449 --> 00:13:27,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, listen to our other 200 and plus something talks, which is all about, again, seeing where the problem is, containing your behavior, curbing your unwholesome sort of engagement with sense objects, sense restraint, obviously reading the suttas and learning more, but

167
00:13:28,454 --> 00:13:29,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Be careful with that.

168
00:13:29,575 --> 00:13:34,560
[SPEAKER_00]: As we said before, like, you can be reading the suttas and all of that just to get rid of the pain of your headaches.

169
00:13:35,701 --> 00:13:36,421
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no, no, no.

170
00:13:36,621 --> 00:13:42,747
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that is to inform you how to keep getting closer to the illness, to the cancer of your own mind.

171
00:13:43,247 --> 00:13:45,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Not to treat you with pain right away.

172
00:13:45,710 --> 00:13:51,194
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha described that for somebody, practice towards enlightenment is going to be slow and painful throughout.

173
00:13:52,355 --> 00:13:55,018
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if he has the right view, he'll see past that pain.

174
00:13:56,733 --> 00:13:59,695
[SPEAKER_00]: He won't measure the success, whether it's painful or not.

175
00:13:59,715 --> 00:14:03,216
[SPEAKER_00]: He'll measure whether the mind is getting more obedient or not.

176
00:14:04,297 --> 00:14:09,480
[SPEAKER_00]: For somebody, if the mind was not as wild, pleasure... practice can be very pleasant, very agreeable.

177
00:14:10,400 --> 00:14:11,981
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, it's still the same training, by the way.

178
00:14:12,001 --> 00:14:17,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sometimes even this is something people like, oh, yeah, I need to have this pleasure and joy, because the Buddha said practice is joyful.

179
00:14:17,584 --> 00:14:19,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's not up to you to decide.

180
00:14:20,486 --> 00:14:22,607
[SPEAKER_00]: That's up to that dog you picked up off the street.

181
00:14:23,824 --> 00:14:29,186
[SPEAKER_00]: To decide whether that dog is intelligent enough, capable enough, whether it can pick the commands quickly.

182
00:14:29,527 --> 00:14:30,607
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's up to your mind.

183
00:14:32,568 --> 00:14:38,431
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's a complete wreck, unrestrained, for years, it's not going to be enjoyable.

184
00:14:39,491 --> 00:14:40,412
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's still the same training.

185
00:14:40,652 --> 00:14:42,313
[SPEAKER_00]: And it will still have the same results if you do it.

186
00:14:42,393 --> 00:14:43,273
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the point.

187
00:14:47,695 --> 00:14:51,857
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as somebody said, there is a difference between training your mind

188
00:14:54,680 --> 00:14:59,182
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the same applies to the mind.

189
00:14:59,923 --> 00:15:18,132
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason for that is exactly like you can be just imposing all the... all the precepts, all the sensory strength, like controlling your environment, constricting it, so there is nothing disturbing coming your way, and then confuse that for training the mind, for taming it.

190
00:15:18,673 --> 00:15:21,114
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, you could just get a wild dog and train it.

191
00:15:22,185 --> 00:15:30,127
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't train it in any way, if you don't learn how to communicate to it, with it, it will still bite you if you come within its range.

192
00:15:32,727 --> 00:15:35,768
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in this case, again, yes, first you have to train it.

193
00:15:35,968 --> 00:15:41,729
[SPEAKER_00]: If the mind is just... even if that dog is completely wild, well, you're certainly not going to train it if he can come and go as it pleases.

194
00:15:42,409 --> 00:15:45,490
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha gave the simile in regard to the elephant, but it was exactly the same thing.

195
00:15:45,550 --> 00:15:51,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you find... first you have to just tie it down with the leather straps, where it can't go as it pleases.

196
00:15:53,020 --> 00:15:54,461
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you realize that's the beginning.

197
00:15:55,422 --> 00:16:08,893
[SPEAKER_00]: So, keeping the precepts and containing myself, putting the chain on my own mind, it's the beginning now, and when I see that it's becoming, like, calmer because of that, okay, then I have to learn how to now discern.

198
00:16:09,684 --> 00:16:10,564
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inclinations.

199
00:16:10,984 --> 00:16:13,165
[SPEAKER_00]: And engage it with opposing ones.

200
00:16:13,325 --> 00:16:22,347
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if it wants to incline towards sensuality, well, I'll present it with dangers and perils and dissatisfaction and all the turmoil I had to go through on account of getting what I wanted.

201
00:16:22,827 --> 00:16:24,028
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'll feed it that.

202
00:16:24,988 --> 00:16:27,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Not, I'll beat it until it turns away.

203
00:16:28,449 --> 00:16:32,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's just trying to get rid of your own thoughts, as we say, which is, again, rooted in craving and ill-will.

204
00:16:33,870 --> 00:16:36,131
[SPEAKER_00]: And, in other words, you're just making your mind even wilder.

205
00:16:37,299 --> 00:16:49,126
[SPEAKER_00]: No, just presented this context and protect the contexts of peril, the danger, the lack of satisfaction, all the similes the Buddha gave about sensuality, until that mind sort of admits, yeah, okay.

206
00:16:49,286 --> 00:16:50,627
[SPEAKER_00]: See, you taught it a lesson.

207
00:16:52,408 --> 00:16:57,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, wants to go towards self-loathing.

208
00:16:57,531 --> 00:17:04,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is... none of this is worthy, all of this... Well, be that as it may, there is a factual liberation from all of it.

209
00:17:05,527 --> 00:17:10,749
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a factual ease, there is a factual nibbāna, there is a factual peace.

210
00:17:12,769 --> 00:17:15,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Present it with that context, when it gets too dark.

211
00:17:16,271 --> 00:17:20,752
[SPEAKER_00]: There is factual benefits of even this limited training you did.

212
00:17:21,472 --> 00:17:30,075
[SPEAKER_00]: There is, again, there is like the the the simple immovability, or the increased immovability of your mind, that comes just with with keeping the precepts.

213
00:17:30,915 --> 00:17:32,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, mind becomes much more stable.

214
00:17:33,488 --> 00:17:35,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Less prone to be irrational, emotional.

215
00:17:35,790 --> 00:17:37,912
[SPEAKER_00]: And you see that, and you know that deep down.

216
00:17:38,513 --> 00:17:42,276
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just that when the mind gets a bit darker, you forget that, and then you let it wallow.

217
00:17:42,737 --> 00:17:44,538
[SPEAKER_00]: So again, you don't beat it to stop.

218
00:17:45,920 --> 00:17:47,441
[SPEAKER_00]: You just present it with the context.

219
00:17:47,461 --> 00:17:52,225
[SPEAKER_00]: But look, if you don't go too far that way, you could develop this equilibrium.

220
00:17:52,245 --> 00:17:57,070
[SPEAKER_00]: You could develop this immeasurable pleasure of peace, which is what you always wanted.

221
00:17:58,160 --> 00:17:59,161
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you train it.

222
00:17:59,502 --> 00:18:02,264
[SPEAKER_00]: When it goes... when it's too tight, you loosen it.

223
00:18:02,304 --> 00:18:03,726
[SPEAKER_00]: When it's too loose, you tighten it.

224
00:18:04,146 --> 00:18:05,308
[SPEAKER_00]: But the work has to be done.

225
00:18:05,708 --> 00:18:12,635
[SPEAKER_00]: How painful that training will be, well, depends on how patient you are, how wild the mind was, and many different factors.

226
00:18:12,755 --> 00:18:14,837
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's really, as I said before, that's not the point.

227
00:18:15,038 --> 00:18:17,140
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is to go through the training procedure.

